2. Mathematical Preliminaries

This chapter fixes notation and states the formal structures manipulated in subsequent proofs. No originality is claimed.

2.1 Language Models and Proof Kernels

Definition 2.1 (Language Model). A language model is a parameterized family P_\theta(x_t \mid x_{<t}) over a vocabulary \mathcal V, \theta \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb R^D. Joint probability factorizes as \prod_t P_\theta(x_t\mid x_{<t}).

Definition 2.2 (Transformer). The architecture of Vaswani et al. (NIPS 2017) using multi‑head scaled dot‑product attention. Only differentiability of the log‑probabilities is required for the purposes of this work.

Definition 2.3 (Cross‑Entropy Loss). Given a corpus \mathcal D, \mathcal L(\theta)=-\frac{1}{|\mathcal D|}\sum_{\mathbf x}\frac{1}{|\mathbf x|}\sum_t \log P_\theta(x_t\mid x_{<t}). Minimizing this loss maximizes the empirical likelihood. Shannon (1948) showed that cross‑entropy directly gives the average code length, and its exponential defines perplexity, the standard intrinsic metric. Perplexity measures distributional similarity—it does not measure truth.

Definition 2.4 (Proof Assistant Kernel). A proof assistant kernel is a deterministic, computable function \mathcal K(t,\Gamma) \in \{0,1\} where t is a candidate proof term and \Gamma a context. \mathcal K(t,\Gamma) = 1 if and only if t type‑checks against the goal proposition in context \Gamma. The Lean 4 kernel implements the Calculus of Inductive Constructions (CIC) (de Moura et al., CADE 2021). The full Lean 4 kernel is approximately 20k lines of code, but the critical type‑checking core—the trusted computing base for soundness—is in the range of 4k–8k lines, as verified by independent external checkers such as lean4lean. This is the TCB we shall rely upon in Chapter 6.

Remark 2.1 (Probability‑Truth Gap). A language model trained on internet text may assign high probability to “227 × 343 = 77861” because the pattern “number × number = number” is prevalent, yet confabulate a wrong product. The model optimizes for distributional similarity, not logical entailment. This gap is the fundamental motivation for neuro‑symbolic architectures.

2.2 Joint Embedding Space

Definition 2.5 (Joint Embedding). Let \mathcal L_{\text{NL}} be the set of natural‑language propositions and \mathcal L_{\text{FL}} the set of formal‑language types (in Lean). A joint embedding space is a Euclidean space \mathcal E = \mathbb R^{d_e} equipped with two encoders \mathcal E_{\text{NL}}: \mathcal L_{\text{NL}} \to \mathcal E and \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}: \mathcal L_{\text{FL}} \to \mathcal E.

Definition 2.6 (Auto‑Formalization by Retrieval). The auto‑formalization mapping f: \mathcal L_{\text{NL}} \to \mathcal L_{\text{FL}} is defined by nearest‑neighbor retrieval in the joint embedding space:

f(\phi) = \arg\min_{\psi \in \mathcal L_{\text{FL}}} \|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi) - \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi)\|.

This design is directly inspired by ProofBridge, which uses such a joint embedding to retrieve semantically related formal examples for translation.

2.3 Proof Environment as an MDP

Definition 2.7 (Proof MDP). The proof environment is modeled as a Markov decision process \mathcal M_{\text{proof}} = (\mathcal S, \mathcal A, T_{\mathcal K}, R) where:

· \mathcal S: states—each state s = (\Gamma, G) is a pair consisting of a context \Gamma of typed assumptions and a goal proposition G.
· \mathcal A: actions—finite set of tactic strings (Lean tactic invocations).
· T_{\mathcal K}(s, a): deterministic transition—the proof assistant kernel executes tactic a in state s and returns either a new goal state or the absorbing terminal state \Omega if all goals are closed.
· R(s, a) = 1 if the action leads to \Omega (complete proof) and 0 otherwise.

The MDP is assumed to have a finite horizon T_{\max} (maximum proof length) and deterministic transitions. This is justified because we restrict to a fixed set of deterministic Lean tactics and a fixed kernel version. The determinism of transitions is critical for the soundness arguments in Chapters 4 and 6.

2.4 Policy Gradient Methods

Definition 2.8 (Policy). A policy \pi_\theta: \mathcal S \to \Delta(\mathcal A) is a parameterized stochastic mapping from states to probability distributions over actions. The policy is implemented by a transformer language model.

Definition 2.9 (Expected Return). J(\theta) = \mathbb E_{\tau \sim \pi_\theta} \big[\sum_{t=0}^{T_{\max}-1} R(s_t, a_t)\big], where \tau = (s_0, a_0, s_1, \dots, s_T) is a trajectory sampled from the policy.

Theorem 2.1 (Policy Gradient Theorem, Sutton et al., NIPS 1999).

\nabla_\theta J(\theta) = \mathbb E_{\pi_\theta}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T_{\max}-1} \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(a_t \mid s_t)\, Q^{\pi_\theta}(s_t, a_t)\right].

Definition 2.10 (REINFORCE). The REINFORCE estimator with baseline b is

\nabla_\theta \hat J(\theta) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(a_t^{(i)} \mid s_t^{(i)})\, (G_t^{(i)} - b).

2.5 Stochastic Approximation

Robbins‑Monro Conditions. (Robbins & Monro, 1951). Let step sizes \eta_k > 0 satisfy \sum_k \eta_k = \infty and \sum_k \eta_k^2 < \infty. Consider the iteration \theta_{k+1} = \theta_k + \eta_k (H(\theta_k) + \xi_k) where H is Lipschitz continuous and \xi_k is a martingale difference noise sequence. Then \theta_k converges almost surely to a compact set where H(\theta) = 0.

Kushner‑Clark Lemma. (Kushner & Yin, 2003). Under the Robbins‑Monro conditions and a drift condition ensuring a Lyapunov function decreases in expectation, the parameter sequence converges almost surely to the zero set of the mean field.

