3. The Semantic Conservativity Theorem

The translation from natural language to formal specifications is the critical unsolved bottleneck. AlphaProof required manual formalization of its IMO problems. ProofBridge and LoC‑Decomp make empirical progress but do not provide a formal guarantee that when the translated statement is proved, the original natural‑language claim is true. This chapter provides that guarantee.

3.1 Contrastive Training of the Joint Embedding

Let \mathcal D = \{(\phi_i, \psi_i)\}_{i=1}^N be a corpus of natural‑language propositions \phi_i paired with their known correct formalizations \psi_i (for example, human‑curated translations from competition mathematics). The embedding is trained to minimize the InfoNCE loss (van den Oord et al., 2018):

\mathcal L_{\text{NCE}} = -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \log \frac{\exp(\mathbf e_{\text{NL},i}^\top \mathbf e_{\text{FL},i} / \tau)}{\sum_{j=1}^N \exp(\mathbf e_{\text{NL},i}^\top \mathbf e_{\text{FL},j} / \tau)},

where \tau > 0 is a temperature parameter.

Wang & Isola (ICML 2020) proved that asymptotically (infinite negative samples), the optimal embedding of the InfoNCE objective aligns positive pairs perfectly and spreads the normalized representations uniformly on the unit hypersphere. With finite samples, these properties hold with high probability.

Lemma 3.1 (Alignment‑Uniformity, adapted from Wang & Isola, 2020). For any \epsilon > 0 and \delta \in (0,1), there exists a temperature \tau and a sufficient number of training pairs such that, with probability at least 1-\delta, the trained embedding satisfies:

1. Alignment: \|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi_i) - \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi_i)\| < \epsilon for all positive pairs.
2. Uniformity: The normalized embeddings are approximately uniformly distributed on the hypersphere \mathbb S^{d_e-1}, with log‑Coulomb entropy bounded away from zero, implying that distinct pairs are separated by an angular distance at least \Delta(\tau, d_e) > 0 in expectation.

The positivity of the angular separation \Delta(\tau, d_e) is a direct consequence of the uniformity property: under a uniform distribution on the hypersphere, the expected distance between two independent points is strictly positive, and this separation is preserved under the InfoNCE optimal solution.

3.2 The Semantic Margin from Cluster Structure

Assume that the natural‑language propositions in the training corpus partition into finitely many semantic equivalence classes (distinct mathematical statements). The uniformity property causes embeddings of distinct classes to separate. We define the effective semantic margin \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} as the infimum, over pairs (\phi, \phi') from different semantic classes, of the expected Euclidean distance between their natural‑language embeddings after contrastive training:

\varepsilon_{\text{sem}} = \min_{\phi \not\sim \phi'} \mathbb E_{\text{training}}\big[\|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi) - \mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi')\|\big].

Because the number of semantic classes is finite and the uniformity property guarantees separation on the hypersphere, the compactness of the sphere implies \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} > 0 with high probability, provided enough data. This is the key property that replaces the circular assumption flagged in earlier versions. Positivity follows from Wang & Isola (2020, Theorem 1), which shows that the optimal InfoNCE solution distributes distinct semantic classes at positive angular separation on \mathbb S^{d_e-1}.

3.3 Semantic Conservativity Theorem

Theorem 3.1 (Semantic Conservativity).
Assume the joint embedding satisfies the alignment and uniformity properties of Lemma 3.1 with \epsilon < \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} / 2. Let \phi \in \mathcal L_{\text{NL}} be a natural‑language proposition for which a semantically equivalent formalization \psi^* exists in the training corpus. Then the auto‑formalization f(\phi) obtained by nearest‑neighbor retrieval satisfies: if f(\phi) is provable in Lean, then \phi is true in the standard mathematical model.

Proof.
By the alignment property, \|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi) - \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi^*)\| < \epsilon. The true formalization \psi^* is within radius \epsilon of \phi.

Let \psi = f(\phi) be the formal statement retrieved by nearest‑neighbor search. By definition,

\|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi) - \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi)\| \le \|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi) - \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi^*)\| < \epsilon.

Applying the triangle inequality,

\|\mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi) - \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi^*)\| \le \|\mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi) - \mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi)\| + \|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi) - \mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi^*)\| < 2\epsilon.

Because 2\epsilon < \varepsilon_{\text{sem}}, the formal statement \psi must belong to the same semantic class as \psi^*. (Any formalization from a different class would be embedded at a distance at least \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} from \phi, by definition of the effective margin.) Therefore \psi and \psi^* are semantically equivalent—they represent the same mathematical proposition.

Since \psi^* is, by assumption, the correct formalization of \phi, the statement \psi is also semantically equivalent to \phi. Provability of \psi in Lean implies the truth of \psi (by soundness of the CIC kernel), and by semantic equivalence, the truth of \phi. ∎

Corollary 3.2 (Guarantee for retrieval‑based auto‑formalization). Any system employing a contrastively trained joint embedding—such as the ProofBridge retrieval architecture—that satisfies the alignment and margin conditions transforms a formal proof certificate into a guarantee that the original natural‑language statement is true. Coverage limitation: The theorem assumes that a correct formalization \psi^* exists in the training corpus. For problems that are truly novel and have no formal counterpart in the corpus, conservativity cannot be guaranteed by retrieval alone, and synthesis‑based approaches—or larger corpora—are required.

