5. Sample Complexity with Dependency‑Aware Hierarchical Decomposition

5.1 Lower Bound for Monolithic Proof Generation

Construction 5.1 (Hidden‑Path Proof Tree). Parameterized by proof depth d and branching factor b. The state space is a full b-ary AND‑OR tree of depth d. At each AND node, exactly one of the b tactic choices is correct; the winning path is selected uniformly at random among the b^d possibilities. The terminal reward is 1 if and only if the exact hidden path is followed.

Theorem 5.1 (Sample Complexity Lower Bound).
Any reinforcement‑learning algorithm interacting with the Hidden‑Path MDP by observing only terminal rewards requires \Omega(b^d) environment episodes to identify the correct path with probability at least 2/3.

Proof.
The construction reduces precisely to a best‑arm identification problem in a K = b^d-armed deterministic bandit, where exactly one arm yields reward 1 and all others yield 0. Bubeck and Cesa‑Bianchi (2012, Theorem 2.3.1) established that any algorithm requires \Omega(K) queries to identify the unique best arm with constant probability. The terminal‑reward observation provides no intermediate feedback, so the reduction is exact. ∎

This exponential dependence on depth is consistent with the empirical observation that whole‑proof generation struggles dramatically as proof length increases.

5.2 Dependency‑Aware Upper Bound

Theorem 5.2 (Hierarchical Decomposition Upper Bound).
Consider a proof of depth d that can be factorized into L lemmas, arranged in m dependency tiers (a directed acyclic graph). Let w_k be the maximum effective branching factor within tier k. A hierarchical reinforcement‑learning agent that learns each lemma independently and composes them in topological order (tier 1, then tier 2, …, tier m) achieves expected return 1-\epsilon using

O\!\left( \sum_{k=1}^m L_k \cdot \mathrm{poly}(d_k) \cdot b^{\,w_k} \right)

environment interactions, where L_k and d_k are the number and maximal proof depth of lemmas in tier k.

Proof sketch.
Within a dependency tier, lemmas are mutually independent, so the total computational cost is the sum of the costs per lemma. For a single lemma of depth d_k and effective branching factor b, the softmax policy gradient in the tabular setting converges to an \epsilon-optimal policy in \mathrm{poly}(d_k) \cdot b^{d_k} iterations (Mei et al., ICML 2020, Theorem 1). When the lemma’s proof search space is narrowed by the dependency constraints—so that only a subset of branches remains viable—the effective depth is reduced to w_k, the width of the dependency‑constrained subspace. Summation across tiers yields the claimed bound.

When lemmas within a tier depend on a small number of prior lemmas (as is typical in the Calculus of Inductive Constructions), the effective branching factor is multiplied by a factor polynomial in the number of prior lemmas, which does not change the asymptotic exponential structure of the bound. ∎

Corollary 5.1 (Explanation of Empirical Hierarchical Gains). When the proof can be decomposed into a linear chain of lemmas (m = d, w_k = 1 for all k), the complexity reduces from \Omega(b^d) to O(d \cdot L \cdot \mathrm{poly}(d)). This exponential‑to‑polynomial transition explains the empirical findings that hierarchical (lemma‑based) proof generation “outperforms standard whole‑proof generation setups in terms of sample efficiency and scalability” and that “RL allows to train proof decomposition policies successfully”.

5.3 Generalization Guarantee

Theorem 5.3 (Generalization via Rademacher Complexity).
For a policy class \Pi induced by a transformer with bounded weight norms, the expected proof‑search error on unseen problems concentrates around the empirical error. The generalization gap is bounded by the empirical Rademacher complexity of the transformer class, which scales as O(1/\sqrt{n}) when the number of training problems n is large (Golowich et al., COLT 2018).

