6. Meta‑Verification: Convergence of the Learning Algorithm

This chapter presents a proof that the policy‑gradient algorithm, under the conditions of our proof environment, converges to a stationary point of the expected return. The proof is provided in a self‑contained mathematical form designed for future mechanization in Isabelle/HOL. No claim is made that a complete formalization currently exists; the proof sketch below is mechanizable, and a partial implementation of the drift condition is under development.

6.1 Smoothness of the Softmax Policy Objective

Lemma 6.1 (Lipschitz Gradient). For a softmax policy over a finite deterministic MDP, J(\theta) is twice continuously differentiable and its gradient is Lipschitz continuous with constant L_J depending on the maximum norm of the feature map and the maximum episode length.

Proof sketch. The softmax function is smooth; the probability of a trajectory is a product of softmax outputs, and the composition preserves smoothness. The Hessian of the expected return is bounded because the Fisher information matrix of the softmax policy has bounded eigenvalues.

6.2 Lyapunov Drift Condition

Lemma 6.2 (Expected Decrease). Define the Lyapunov function V(\theta) = -J(\theta). Under the Robbins‑Monro step‑size conditions and the Lipschitz gradient property (Lemma 6.1), with an unbiased baseline,

\mathbb E[V(\theta_{k+1}) - V(\theta_k) \mid \theta_k] \le -\eta_k \|\nabla J(\theta_k)\|^2 + \eta_k^2 C,

for some constant C depending on the Lipschitz constant and the variance of the gradient estimator.

6.3 Convergence Theorem

Theorem 6.1 (Almost‑Sure Convergence). Under the conditions of Lemmas 6.1 and 6.2 and the Robbins‑Monro step‑size schedule, \theta_k converges almost surely to a compact set where \nabla J(\theta) = 0.

Proof sketch. The Lyapunov drift condition of Lemma 6.2 provides the required decrease in expectation. The gradient estimator noise is a martingale difference sequence with bounded second moments. The Kushner‑Clark lemma (Kushner & Yin, 2003) guarantees that the interpolated continuous‑time process tracks the gradient flow \dot\theta = \nabla J(\theta), and converges to the zero set of \nabla J.

6.4 The Trusted Computing Base

Corollary 6.2 (Minimal TCB). The only software components that must be trusted for logical soundness of the entire neuro‑symbolic theorem‑proving system are:

1. The Lean 4 kernel (type‑checking core ≈ 4k–8k lines of C++),
2. The Isabelle/HOL kernel (≈ 2k lines of ML, as estimated from the seL4 verification; Klein et al., SOSP 2009).

The billions of parameters of the language model, the entire reinforcement‑learning training loop, and the auto‑formalization pipeline are untrusted. Any error that would produce an unsound proof term is rejected by the kernel at verification time. Errors of incompleteness—the failure to find any proof—cannot be detected by the kernel alone, but they do not compromise soundness: the system either produces a certified proof or produces nothing.

