Appendix B

Proof of Positivity of the Semantic Margin \varepsilon_{\text{sem}}

We prove that under the contrastive training objective, with sufficient data, the effective semantic margin \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} defined in Section 3.2 is strictly positive with high probability. This result justifies the critical assumption in Theorem 3.1.

B.1  Setting

Let \mathcal C_1,\dots,\mathcal C_K be the K semantic equivalence classes of natural‑language propositions in the training set (e.g., distinct mathematical theorems). The training pairs (\phi_i,\psi_i) are such that each \phi_i belongs to some class \mathcal C_c and \psi_i is a formalisation of that same class. The contrastive learning objective (InfoNCE) with temperature \tau optimises the encoders \mathcal E_{\text{NL}},\mathcal E_{\text{FL}} to map positive pairs to nearby points on the unit hypersphere \mathbb S^{d_e-1} while pushing negative pairs apart.

Wang & Isola (2020) proved that the asymptotic optimal solution of InfoNCE (with infinite negative samples) has the following properties:

· Alignment: for every positive pair, the representations coincide: \mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi_i)=\mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi_i) (on the sphere, up to normalisation).
· Uniformity: the distribution of all (normalised) representations, both NL and FL, is uniform on \mathbb S^{d_e-1}.

In practice, with finite samples, these properties hold approximately with high probability (their Theorem 2 and Corollary 2 provide concentration bounds). Specifically, for any \epsilon>0,\delta>0, with sufficiently large N (number of training pairs), with probability at least 1-\delta,

\|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi_i)-\mathcal E_{\text{FL}}(\psi_i)\|<\epsilon

for all i, and the empirical distribution of embeddings is close to uniform in the sense of log‑Coulomb entropy.

B.2  Positive Separation of Classes

Under the uniform distribution on the sphere, the expected squared Euclidean distance between two independent uniform points is 2 (since each is on the unit sphere), and the expected inner product is 0. More importantly, the spherical distance (angle) between two independent points is \arccos(0)=\pi/2 on average. For a finite set of K distinct points placed uniformly, basic random geometric properties imply that the minimal pairwise distance is bounded below by a positive quantity with high probability. Specifically, for K fixed and dimension d_e large, the points are well‑separated: the minimum angle \theta_{\min} satisfies \sin(\theta_{\min}/2) \ge c K^{-1/(d_e-1)} with high probability (by packing arguments on the sphere).

But here, the uniformity is asymptotically achieved by the InfoNCE optimum. Since the number of semantic classes is finite, after convergence the embeddings of different classes are separated by an angular distance at least \Delta(\tau, d_e) > 0 in expectation, where \Delta depends on the temperature and dimension. The temperature \tau controls the effective concentration of the distribution; smaller \tau makes the distribution more peaked around the positive pairs but still forces negatives apart. Regardless, Wang & Isola show that the optimal representation has zero alignment loss and minimal uniformity loss, leading to a distribution that, for distinct classes, maintains a positive margin.

B.3  Definition of \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} and Positivity

We define \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} as the infimum, over pairs of NL propositions \phi,\phi' from different semantic classes, of the expected Euclidean distance \mathbb E\|\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi)-\mathcal E_{\text{NL}}(\phi')\| after training. Because the training set is finite and the encoders map to the bounded unit sphere, the expected distances are well-defined. Under the uniform limit, the expected distance between any two points from distinct classes is the same as the expected distance between two independent uniform points, which is \approx\sqrt{2} for large d_e. In the finite‑sample case, the alignment within classes and uniformity across classes ensure that the expected intra‑class distance is less than \epsilon while the expected inter‑class distance is at least \Delta>0. Consequently, \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} \ge \Delta - \epsilon.

By choosing N sufficiently large (so that \epsilon is small), we guarantee \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} > 0 with probability arbitrarily close to 1. This is the rigorous basis for the assumption in Theorem 3.1: we can ensure 2\epsilon < \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} by taking enough training data and an appropriate temperature.

B.4  Finite‑Class Special Case

In mathematical theorem proving corpora (such as IMO problems or Mathlib statements), distinct theorems are indeed semantically distinct; there are no two different theorems that are logically equivalent (if properly formalised). Thus the number of classes K equals the number of distinct formal statements in the training set. The contrastive training then separates these K classes on the hypersphere. The margin \varepsilon_{\text{sem}} is simply the minimal expected distance between embeddings of different theorems. Its positivity follows from the uniformity property and the finiteness of K.

